1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,898
Welcome to System Design with Java. This sample walks through the learning package using

2
00:00:05,898 --> 00:00:11,796
small, inspectable examples. Begin with requirements, not a list of technologies. For checkout, ask

3
00:00:11,796 --> 00:00:17,695
what a customer can do, how much traffic to expect, and which rules must

4
00:00:17,695 --> 00:00:23,593
always remain true. One logical checkout should create one order. A timeout should not

5
00:00:23,593 --> 00:00:29,491
cause a second charge. The course develops these ideas from a single process to

6
00:00:29,491 --> 00:00:35,389
distributed workflows. The supplied code is for learning, and advanced production integrations are exercises.

7
00:00:36,589 --> 00:00:41,218
Run the capacity lab. One million daily users making twenty requests each produce about

8
00:00:41,218 --> 00:00:45,847
two hundred thirty one requests per second on average. Assuming a tenfold peak gives

9
00:00:45,847 --> 00:00:50,475
about two thousand three hundred fifteen. With a mean total response time of two

10
00:00:50,475 --> 00:00:55,104
hundred milliseconds, Little’s Law gives about four hundred sixty three requests in flight in

11
00:00:55,104 --> 00:00:59,733
a stable system. These are estimates under stated assumptions. They do not tell you

12
00:00:59,733 --> 00:01:04,362
how many servers you need until you measure what each server and its dependencies

13
00:01:04,362 --> 00:01:08,990
can handle.

14
00:01:10,190 --> 00:01:15,499
Run the cache lab. The first read returns a stored price. At the five

15
00:01:15,499 --> 00:01:20,807
second expiry boundary, the same lookup returns null. The test advances a fake monotonic

16
00:01:20,807 --> 00:01:26,116
clock, so it does not need to sleep. Read TtlCache dot java to see

17
00:01:26,116 --> 00:01:31,424
a bounded, synchronized least recently used cache. A production cache also needs a plan

18
00:01:31,424 --> 00:01:36,733
for concurrent misses, stale data, and invalidation across replicas. Never assume that a fast

19
00:01:36,733 --> 00:01:42,041
cached value is authoritative enough for every business decision.

20
00:01:43,241 --> 00:01:48,629
The token bucket starts with three tokens and refills at two per second. Three

21
00:01:48,629 --> 00:01:54,018
immediate requests succeed, and the fourth is rejected. After half a second, one token

22
00:01:54,018 --> 00:01:59,406
is available again. The synchronized method makes refill and consumption atomic inside one Java

23
00:01:59,406 --> 00:02:04,794
process. Four replicas with independent buckets do not enforce one shared customer quota. To

24
00:02:04,794 --> 00:02:10,182
extend the example, define tenant-scoped keys, an atomic shared operation, and the behavior when

25
00:02:10,182 --> 00:02:15,570
the quota store is unavailable.

26
00:02:16,770 --> 00:02:21,929
Run the hashing lab. It assigns ten thousand keys to three nodes, adds a

27
00:02:21,929 --> 00:02:27,087
fourth node, and counts changed owners. Virtual nodes help spread placement, but the result

28
00:02:27,087 --> 00:02:32,246
is not perfectly balanced and a popular key can still be hot. The test

29
00:02:32,246 --> 00:02:37,404
verifies that keys moved by adding D now belong to D. This code is

30
00:02:37,404 --> 00:02:42,563
only a routing model. A real resharding operation must also copy data, handle writes

31
00:02:42,563 --> 00:02:47,721
during movement, transfer ownership safely, and verify that nothing was lost.

32
00:02:48,921 --> 00:02:53,763
The replication lab writes one item, copies it to a follower, and then updates

33
00:02:53,763 --> 00:02:58,604
the leader to two items. A follower read is stale. A read-your-writes helper requires

34
00:02:58,604 --> 00:03:03,445
a minimum version and falls back to the leader. Later, an old delivery is

35
00:03:03,445 --> 00:03:08,286
ignored rather than overwriting a newer value. This is a deterministic in-memory model, not

36
00:03:08,286 --> 00:03:13,128
a consensus implementation. In a real system, you must also decide what to do

37
00:03:13,128 --> 00:03:17,969
when the leader cannot be reached and the follower is behind.

38
00:03:19,169 --> 00:03:23,811
A client can time out after the server commits an order but before receiving

39
00:03:23,811 --> 00:03:28,453
the reply. The next attempt must reuse the same logical checkout key. OrderStore records

40
00:03:28,453 --> 00:03:33,095
the order, the key, and an outbox event inside one synchronized operation. A matching

41
00:03:33,095 --> 00:03:37,738
retry returns the same order. A different amount with that key is rejected. The

42
00:03:37,738 --> 00:03:42,380
core tests run one hundred concurrent attempts and verify one order and one event.

43
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:47,022
All of this state is in memory, so a durable database is the next

44
00:03:47,022 --> 00:03:51,664
extension.

45
00:03:52,864 --> 00:03:58,726
Run the outbox lab. The consumer applies an event, but the relay is treated

46
00:03:58,726 --> 00:04:04,588
as interrupted before acknowledgement. The same event is delivered again, and the toy effect

47
00:04:04,588 --> 00:04:10,450
count remains one. Notice the boundary: consumer memory stays alive. This does not prove

48
00:04:10,450 --> 00:04:16,312
crash-safe exactly-once behavior. A durable consumer needs its event identifier and database effect in

49
00:04:16,312 --> 00:04:22,174
the same transaction. External providers need their own idempotency and reconciliation design. Always say

50
00:04:22,174 --> 00:04:28,036
exactly which effect and failure model a guarantee covers.

51
00:04:29,236 --> 00:04:34,720
The circuit breaker opens after two failures and rejects the next call. After cooldown,

52
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:40,203
one successful probe closes it. A generation number prevents a late result from an

53
00:04:40,203 --> 00:04:45,687
older call from closing a newer open state. The tests also check that only

54
00:04:45,687 --> 00:04:51,170
one recovery probe runs. A breaker does not impose a timeout by itself. Combine

55
00:04:51,170 --> 00:04:56,653
bounded calls with retry budgets, jitter, and admission control. Virtual threads make waiting cheaper,

56
00:04:56,653 --> 00:05:02,137
but they do not create database connections or provider capacity.

57
00:05:03,337 --> 00:05:08,838
Start with the build script, run all ten labs, and inspect the source beside

58
00:05:08,838 --> 00:05:14,339
the guide. The full test script also checks the HTTP capstone on your machine.

59
00:05:14,339 --> 00:05:19,841
During package creation, thirty core checks passed, while the environment blocked listening sockets, so

60
00:05:19,841 --> 00:05:25,342
network integration was not run. The guide contains twenty four modules and eight design

61
00:05:25,342 --> 00:05:30,843
cases, plus video links for deeper study. Your final project adds durable storage, a

62
00:05:30,843 --> 00:05:36,344
real event pipeline, authorization, observability, and measured recovery. Explain what each test proves and

63
00:05:36,344 --> 00:05:41,846
what remains an assumption.
